!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/34/ltr8/http/meta-service-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/meta-kernel.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/meta.tn"
@doc:"""
  A meta layer for describing a service: what it can be asked to do (methods, grouped into interfaces)
  and how one transport exposes that (HTTP operations). A schema governed by this one is a service
  description, and its entries are methods, operations, or a mix.

  One vocabulary, two constructors, and the shared part is an ordinary record. `signature` is the
  transport-neutral contract: request, response, errors, and the two facts a caller composing calls
  needs and has no verb to read them from (safe, idempotent). `method` is a signature and nothing
  else; `operation` is a signature plus an HTTP binding. Both are `~data & signature ...`, the shape
  §4.2 sanctions -- a base kind seeds the constructor level and a record mixin lends vocabulary, as
  `uri_type => ~text_type & atom_specification & { ... }` does.

  Why not `operation => method & { ... }`: `method` is `kind: DATA`, and §4.1 makes a DATA entry as a
  composition operand a resolver error -- the same rule that refuses `getOrder => fetch<order>`.
  A DATA entry can be declared and applied, never named. So the two views relate by ERASURE, not by
  reference: an operation IS a method with binding fields added, and the interface view of a service
  is its methods plus its operations with those fields dropped. A developer offering both never
  writes a method twice, and the two cannot disagree because there is one declaration.

  Two entries a governed schema writes, and both are MAPS:

    orders     => !interface { methods: { place_order => !method { ... }  ... } }
    orders_api => !api { implements: [orders]
                         resources: { "/orders" => !resource { POST => !operation { method: place_order ... } } } }

  Methods are keys inside their interface, and operations are values two maps down -- keyed by path,
  then by verb -- so neither is a global entry. That is what the flat namespace was costing: two
  interfaces may both declare `place_order`, an api needs no invented operation names, and an
  interface is one entry with a name, a `@doc` and an `extends`. It works today because the
  implementation admits a DATA CONSTRUCTOR as a map's value type (an INSTANCE is still refused at a type
  position); [TSON-SCHEMA] §4.1's text reads as if it should not, and the experiment's README records
  that as the question to settle before building on it.

  Three ways to use it:
    interface only    an `!interface`; nothing HTTP in the document.
    web service only  an `!api` whose operations carry their signatures INLINE.
    both              an `!interface`, and an `!api` that `implements` it, its operations naming methods.
                      An operation names a method OR carries a signature, never both.

  Everything that relates the two -- a `method` name, an `implements`, a `{segment}` in a path -- is an
  identifier the resolver does not check: a DATA entry cannot be referenced, and a map key is data. The
  description reader checks all of it at startup in the merged namespace (`Routes`, in the experiment's
  Java), which is the same tier as coverage. When the kernel grows a reference form for data entries,
  those slots change type and that half of the check deletes.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    The transport-neutral contract of one callable thing. A plain record on purpose: it is what `method`
    and an inline `operation` share, and the only kind of entry a DATA constructor may compose with.

    `request` absent means the call takes nothing; `response` absent means it returns nothing (an HTTP
    204). `errors` lists BUSINESS errors -- the request was schema-valid and the domain said no -- each
    a type composing `problem`. A transport's own refusals (a body failing its schema, an Accept it
    cannot satisfy) are not listed: every call has them and the error boundary answers them.

    `safe` and `idempotent` live here rather than being read off an HTTP verb because the caller that
    needs them -- an agent planning a batch, deciding what may be retried or reordered -- has no verb.
    `safe` implies `idempotent`. The HTTP projection CHECKS its verb against them (GET must be safe;
    PUT and DELETE idempotent); it never derives one from the other.

    `request_stream` and `response_stream` say that the request or the response is a SEQUENCE of
    documents of the declared type -- count unknown in advance, each one complete and actionable as it
    arrives -- rather than one document. Together they encode the four call shapes: unary, server
    streaming, client streaming, bidirectional. A stream is a property of the CALL, not of the type:
    `response: order  response_stream: true` is not `response: [order]`. A list is one value that
    arrives whole and has a length; a stream's element boundaries are interaction boundaries, which is
    the one thing a type cannot say and the reason the flags live here rather than in the types. Nor is
    it chunked transfer or a large document read incrementally -- every call has that, and it is the
    transport's.

    What the format lacks for a stream to be real is a framing for a sequence of documents: a TSON
    document is one value and a reader rejects trailing content. Until one exists `operation` pins
    both false; the candidate framings are recorded in the experiment's README rather than decided
    here. A transport with no framing question -- an in-process plan executor -- may honour the flags
    today.
    """
  signature => {
    request:          type_ref?
    response:         type_ref?
    errors:           [type_ref]?
    safe:             boolean ~ false
    idempotent:       boolean ~ false
    request_stream:   boolean ~ false
    response_stream:  boolean ~ false
  }

  @doc:"A method: a signature and nothing more. Its NAME is the key it sits under in an interface."
  method => ~data & signature

  @doc:"""
    An interface: a named, documented map of methods. A method is a map VALUE under its name, so its
    name is scoped to the interface and a `@doc` written before the key documents it.

    `extends` names other interfaces, whose methods this one also has; the reader walks it and refuses
    a cycle or a name that is not an interface. It is not `!!import`: importing a document brings its
    entries into the namespace, where extending an interface says whose methods are also mine.
    """
  interface => ~data & {
    extends:  [type_name]?
    methods:  {type_name => method}
  }

  @doc:"Verb, not method: `method` is the constructor above, and `{ method: POST }` helps nobody."
  http_verb => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"Refined, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

  @doc:"""
    Where a request record's fields go, and the place where an interface and the web service over it
    are allowed to look different. The verb and the path are not here: they are the keys an operation
    sits under, in its resource and its api.

    A method has one request record, and the operation distributes that record's fields across the HTTP
    request: a `{segment}` in the resource's path binds the field of that name; `query` names fields
    carried as query parameters; `headers` maps a field to the header that carries it; `body` names ONE
    field carried as the whole body, unwrapped. Whatever is left is the remainder, and the remainder is
    the body -- unless the verb is GET or HEAD, or `body` already named a field, in which case it is the
    query. Every field lands in exactly one place, and a field carried in the path, the query or a
    header must be a scalar -- a URL segment cannot carry a record. That is what makes the mapping
    checkable: a field placed twice, a name the request does not declare, or a container in the path is
    a bad operation rather than a surprise.

    There is no parameter list. A parameter-only endpoint declares a one-field request record
    (`schema_ref => { schemaPath: text }`) and binds it from the path -- every method has a request
    record, which is what lets one rule serve every endpoint and the check see every field.
    """
  placement => {
    query:    [field_name]?
    headers:  {field_name => text}?
    body:     field_name?
  }

  @doc:"""
    One HTTP operation: a value under its verb, under its path, in an api. It has a signature ONE of two
    ways -- `method` names one declared on an interface the api implements (or the one `interface`
    names, when two implemented interfaces declare the name), or the signature is written inline for an
    api with no interface behind it. Both at once, or neither, is a bad operation; the reader refuses it.

    `status` is the status of `response`; there is no list of responses, because a method has one
    response type. An error's status is the `status` member its own type pins --
    `sku_not_found => problem & { status: = 404  sku: text }` -- so an error type an operation names
    MUST fix it. That is a conformance check on the service, not a resolver check.

    Long description is a `@doc` before the verb key; `summary` is the short form. The two stream flags
    are pinned false here: the format has no framing for a sequence of documents yet.

    Deliberately absent, each a decision: security schemes, response headers, links, callbacks,
    examples, tags, servers, multi-media-type negotiation, and more than one success response. Adding
    one is a later version under §3.5 once this is published, never an edit.
    """
  operation => ~data & signature & placement & {
    method:           type_name?
    interface:        type_name?
    status:           status_code ~ 200
    summary:          text?
    request_stream:   = false
    response_stream:  = false
  }

  @doc:"One path: its operations, keyed by verb. `!resource { POST => !operation { ... } }`."
  resource => ~data & {
    operations: {http_verb => operation}
  }

  @doc:"""
    A web service: resources keyed by path template, each holding operations keyed by verb -- the shape
    OpenAPI's `paths` has, arrived at from the key types rather than copied. A path key is DATA (a text),
    which is why an operation needs no invented name and why `/orders/{id}` needs no identifier minted
    from it.

    `implements` names the interfaces this api binds, and is a CLAIM the reader checks: every method of
    every implemented interface (through `extends`) has an operation naming it, or an entry in
    `not_bound` giving the reason it does not -- an operation documented ahead of being built, or served
    by another process behind the same proxy. The reason is required because an exemption somebody has
    to justify is a decision, where a boolean is a hole. An api with no `implements` is a web service on
    its own terms, its operations inline.
    """
  api => ~data & {
    implements:  [type_name]?
    not_bound:   {type_name => text}?
    resources:   {text => resource}
  }
}
